<img src="https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1192/format:webp/1*QPToCpZWI8erpb64jhzsBQ.png" width="400px" />
<br />
Illustration from <a href="https://medium.com/adservio/what-is-microbenchmarking-8e6ea189da2d">Adservio's description of the term microbenchmark</a>.

Microbenchmarks in the sense as Rust uses it for example are small applications which 
measure the throughput of individual routines as opposed to the whole application in 
one go. In the context of ExaHyPE, this typically means that we measure the throughput
of one kernel update (update one cell), which explains why a lot of our papers and 
docu use the term kernel benchmark rather than microbenchmark. At the end of the day,
a kernel benchmark is a special type of a microbenchmark.


## Historical remarks and updated design patterns

In the first two generations of ExaHyPE, kernel benchmarks came along as stand-alone
C++ codes, which we could call a driver: They set up the whole Peano/ExaHyPE environment.
Instead of a time stepping loop, they then however measure the time spent in one 
routine (often for various parameters) and then dumps this output. This leads to the 
scenario that we had many different C++ main functions for bespoke microbenchmarks.

As a lot of these main main functions host repeating content, it makes sense to 
consolidate them in one C++ "template" or utility collection to avoid redundant
code. From here, it is only a small step to extend these utilities with a command line
interface to make it easy to test and benchmark various configurations over a microkernel.

This breaks another design pattern of Peano/ExaHyPE which is the idea that we keep 
all user interaction and configurability within the Python API. Therefore, the
latest ExaHyPE rewrite advocates for an altered approach to ship microbenchmarks. 


## Modelling a microbenchmark in Python

The key idea behind a microbenchmark is that an ExaHyPE project uses a dedicated generator
